    <body>
      <sec id="article-28073.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Pulseless electrical activity (PEA), also known as electromechanical dissociation, is a form of cardiac arrest characterized by organized cardiac electrical activity without the generation of a mechanical pulse or sufficient arterial pressure to perfuse vital organs.&#x000a0;This condition&#x000a0;can be further dichotomized into true PEA, characterized by the absence of mechanical cardiac activity, and pseudo-PEA, characterized by mechanical cardiac activity that is inadequate to generate arterial pressure sufficient to produce a palpable pulse. Whether pseudo-PEA should be managed differently from true PEA is a matter of clinical debate. Outcomes associated with PEA are poor compared with cardiac arrest presenting with ventricular fibrillation or ventricular tachycardia.</p>
        <p>PEA is frequently caused by reversible conditions commonly recalled using the mnemonic &#x0201c;5 Hs and 4 Ts.&#x0201d; The 5 Hs include hypoxia, hypovolemia, hyperkalemia or hypokalemia, hypothermia, and acidosis (hydrogen ion excess). The 4 Ts include thrombosis (pulmonary or coronary), cardiac tamponade, toxins, and tension pneumothorax. Treatment of PEA requires rapid recognition and prompt diagnosis of the underlying etiologies.</p>
        <p>This activity for healthcare professionals is designed to sharpen learners' skills in evaluating and managing PEA. Participants will deepen their understanding of the condition's etiology, risk factors, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, and evidence-based diagnostic and therapeutic recommendations. Enhanced competence will empower clinicians to collaborate with interprofessional teams providing care for individuals with this potentially fatal cardiac abnormality.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify pulseless electrical activity based on electrocardiographic findings and clinical presentation.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Determine the etiology of pulseless electrical activity by systematically evaluating for reversible conditions using established clinical frameworks, such as the 5 Hs and 4 Ts.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Develop clinician competence in managing pulseless electrical activity by integrating evidence-based interventions and optimizing rapid, structured resuscitation during cardiac arrest.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate with all members of the interprofessional team, including specialists such as emergency medicine practitioners, intensivists, and trauma surgeons, to ensure prompt and effective resuscitation of patients with pulseless electrical activity.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=28073&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=28073">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28073.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Pulseless electrical activity (PEA), previously known as electromechanical dissociation, is a clinical subset of cardiac arrest characterized by unresponsiveness and absence of a palpable pulse despite the detection of organized cardiac electrical activity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r1">[1]</xref> PEA and asystole comprise the &#x0201c;nonshockable&#x0201d; etiologies of cardiac arrest and&#x000a0;may be contrasted with the &#x0201c;shockable&#x0201d; etiologies, ventricular tachycardia and ventricular fibrillation. Management of PEA is directed toward the identification and treatment of the underlying etiology.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r2">[2]</xref></p>
        <p>PEA was first described in humans in the 1930s based on a study of electrocardiographic changes in the dying heart by Hanson, Purks, and Anderson. PEA is not a primary cardiac event, but it results from failure of the brain, lungs, or vascular system.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>During PEA, ventricular mechanical activity may be absent (true PEA) or present but insufficient to generate arterial pressure adequate to produce a palpable pulse and perfuse end organs (pseudo-PEA). True PEA represents a state of cardiac arrest due to absent mechanical activity and is managed accordingly. Pseudo-PEA, initially managed in the same manner, represents a state of profound shock caused by severe dehydration or obstruction of cardiac output.&#x000a0;Pseudo-PEA frequently progresses to true PEA&#x000a0;when not promptly recognized and treated.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>PEA can present with any organized cardiac rhythm, including supraventricular, junctional, or ventricular rhythms. Frequently observed characteristics include wide-complex rhythms, bradycardias, sinus rhythm, and atrial fibrillation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r5">[5]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28073.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>PEA may present as the initial rhythm in cardiac arrest or as a subsequent rhythm following a period of ventricular tachycardia, ventricular fibrillation, asystole, or return of spontaneous circulation (ROSC).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r6">[6]</xref> Causes of PEA&#x000a0;may be recalled using the mnemonic &#x0201c;Hs and Ts,&#x0201d; which include the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Hs
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Hypovolemia, as in sepsis, hemorrhage, or dehydration</p></list-item><list-item><p>Hypoxia</p></list-item><list-item><p>Hydrogen ion excess (acidosis)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Hypokalemia, hyperkalemia,&#x000a0;or other electrolyte abnormalities</p></list-item><list-item><p>Hypothermia</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Ts
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Tension pneumothorax</p></list-item><list-item><p>Tamponade</p></list-item><list-item><p>Toxins, including &#x003b2;-blockers, calcium channel blockers (CCBs), and sodium-channel blockers</p></list-item><list-item><p>Thrombosis, pulmonary (pulmonary embolism)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Thrombosis, coronary (myocardial infarction) (Source: <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://cpr.heart.org/en/resuscitation-science/cpr-and-ecc-guidelines/algorithms">American Heart Association</ext-link>, 2025)</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Any of the listed etiologies above can result in PEA.&#x000a0;However, cardiac arrest caused by hypoxia or pulmonary embolism is particularly associated with PEA as the predominant initial presenting rhythm. Hypoglycemia can also precipitate PEA.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r7">[7]</xref>&#x000a0;Although not included in the mnemonic, cardiac arrest associated with intracranial hemorrhage has been observed with PEA as the presenting rhythm.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r8">[8]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28073.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>In the US, PEA accounts for approximately 20% to 25% of out-of-hospital cardiac arrest cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r10">[10]</xref> PEA comprises a larger proportion of in-hospital cardiac arrest cases, with estimates ranging from 30% to 65%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r11">[11]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r13">[13]</xref> Factors associated with increased likelihood of PEA as the initial rhythm in cardiac arrest include older age, female sex, and underlying comorbidities, such as pulmonary disease.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r15">[15]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28073.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>PEA occurs when a significant physiologic stressor prevents cardiac contraction from generating an adequate stroke volume in response to electrical depolarization. The inciting events and, therefore, the underlying pathophysiology, can vary.&#x000a0;However, each stressor ultimately causes failure of cardiac pump function through decreased preload, decreased contractility, increased afterload, or a combination of these factors.</p>
        <p>For example, acute coronary ischemia reduces oxygen delivery, leading to myocardial dysfunction. Impaired contractility decreases stroke volume and causes a rapid loss of cardiac output, resulting in severely reduced systemic perfusion and PEA.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r16">[16]</xref> In another scenario, cardiac tamponade produces extrinsic ventricular compression, which decreases preload and reduces stroke volume. The consequent loss of cardiac output severely reduces systemic perfusion and results in PEA.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r17">[17]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28073.s6" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>The relevant patient history should be reviewed during&#x000a0;active resuscitation to identify potential contributors to PEA. Particular attention should be given to severe fluid loss, such as from diarrhea, vomiting, or burns; preceding infections; traumatic or atraumatic hemorrhage,&#x000a0;with manifestations such as gastrointestinal bleeding; and thoracoabdominal trauma. Risk factors for myocardial infarction or pulmonary embolism should be assessed. Any preceding respiratory or apnea events, including opioid overdose, severe asthma or chronic obstructive pulmonary disease exacerbations, anaphylaxis, strangulation, or positional asphyxia, may be relevant.</p>
        <p>Environmental exposures to extreme temperatures and conditions predisposing to metabolic derangements, such as end-stage renal disease, gastrointestinal losses, or use of angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors, should be evaluated. Medication history, including intake of CCBs, &#x003b2;-blockers, or sodium channel blockers, along with environmental toxin exposures, such as cyanide in a house fire, and evidence of recreational drug use,&#x000a0;such as opioid-induced respiratory arrest, may provide additional clues. A history of self-harm,&#x000a0;for example, through&#x000a0;intentional medication ingestion, should also be considered.</p>
        <p>A focused physical examination during resuscitation should&#x000a0;search for indicators of the underlying cause of PEA. Evidence of trauma, particularly to the thorax, should be assessed. Unilateral absence of breath sounds or tracheal deviation may suggest tension pneumothorax or hemothorax. Jugular venous distension may indicate tension pneumothorax, hemothorax, or cardiac tamponade. Signs of hemorrhage should be sought. Indicators of deep venous thrombosis, including unilateral leg swelling or erythema, may suggest pulmonary embolism. Clues to underlying comorbidities, such as the presence of an arteriovenous fistula in a patient on dialysis or evidence of injection drug use, may further guide identification of reversible causes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28073.s7" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>While cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) is ongoing, pertinent monitoring parameters should include cardiac rhythm, oxygen saturation, end-tidal carbon dioxide, core temperature, and blood pressure. Rapid testing, when feasible, should include serum or whole blood electrolytes to evaluate for metabolic etiologies, such as hypokalemia,&#x000a0;hyperkalemia,&#x000a0;or acidosis. Point-of-care ultrasound (POCUS) enables rapid assessment of potential causes during CPR. POCUS can evaluate for pericardial tamponade, volume status (with inferior vena cava diameter as a relative indicator), pneumothorax or hemothorax, intra-abdominal hemorrhage, aortic aneurysm or dissection, and pulmonary embolism manifestations, including deep venous thrombosis and right ventricular strain.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r18">[18]</xref></p>
        <p>When POCUS is performed during the resuscitation of an individual with PEA, clinicians should note that transthoracic echocardiography (TTE) during pulse check pauses has been associated with prolonged interruptions of chest compressions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r19">[19]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r20">[20]</xref> Prolonged pauses are linked to increased cardiac arrest mortality, necessitating careful minimization of compression interruptions while obtaining POCUS data.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r21">[21]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r23">[23]</xref> Implementing a protocolized approach for POCUS during cardiac arrest may help mitigate these deleterious effects.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r24">[24]</xref></p>
        <p>POCUS transesophageal echocardiography (TEE) is an emerging modality for evaluating cardiac arrest, including PEA, and can identify reversible causes such as cardiac tamponade and pulmonary embolism without interrupting chest compressions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r25">[25]</xref>&#x000a0;Both POCUS TTE and TEE can differentiate true PEA from pseudo-PEA. Detection of organized mechanical cardiac activity in the absence of a palpable pulse indicates pseudo-PEA. Carotid pulse waveform assessment via Doppler has also been utilized to indicate low-flow mechanical cardiac output. Arterial line monitoring provides additional evaluation of effective cardiac output by detecting measurable arterial waveforms and blood pressure.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28073.s8" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>
<bold>Initial Management: High-Quality Cardiopulmonary Resuscitation</bold>
</p>
        <p>Initial management of PEA is well summarized by the Advanced Cardiac Life Support (ACLS) algorithm (see <bold>Image. </bold>Adult Advanced Cardiovascular Life Support Algorithm). Immediate high-quality chest compressions should remain the priority. Compressions must be performed at the correct anatomical site while maintaining the appropriate rate and depth, allowing full chest recoil, minimizing interruptions, and mitigating compressor fatigue. Chest compressions should be accompanied by intravenous or intraosseous administration of epinephrine every 3 to 5 minutes, or approximately every 2 cycles of CPR.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r26">[26]</xref></p>
        <p>Coordination of the resuscitation is essential. A team leader should be designated, remaining hands-off from the patient when resources permit, to ensure smooth resuscitation, consideration of underlying causes, and avoidance of potential pitfalls.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r27">[27]</xref></p>
        <p>Targeted management should commence immediately once an underlying cause is identified. Interventions may include decompression of pneumothorax, pericardiocentesis for cardiac tamponade, fluid or blood administration for hypovolemia or hemorrhage, calcium or bicarbonate administration for hyperkalemia or toxin exposure, aggressive rewarming for hypothermia, or thrombolytic therapy for myocardial infarction or pulmonary embolism.</p>
        <p>For PEA secondary to a clear traumatic cause, resuscitative thoracotomy allows rapid management of tension pneumothorax, hemothorax, and pericardial tamponade. This procedure also enables more aggressive resuscitative interventions, including manual cardiac compression, direct temporization or repair of open cardiac lacerations, and, in selected cases, cross-clamping of the aorta to control severe hemorrhage.</p>
        <p>The Western Trauma Association recommends resuscitative thoracotomy in patients who have sustained blunt trauma with less than 10 minutes of prehospital CPR, penetrating torso trauma with less than 15 minutes of CPR, penetrating trauma to the neck or extremities with less than 5 minutes of prehospital CPR, and profound refractory shock.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r28">[28]</xref>&#x000a0;In pediatric patients, a joint guideline from multiple US trauma societies recommends that children&#x000a0;younger than 15 years presenting with traumatic cardiac arrest and signs of life, including an organized cardiac rhythm or cardiac motion on POCUS (ie, PEA), should receive airway management, bleeding control, transfusion, and chest decompression via needle or finger thoracostomy. Resuscitative thoracotomy is recommended if the patient fails to improve or deteriorates.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r29">[29]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Finger-stick blood glucose determination, blood gas analysis, and serum electrolyte&#x000a0;measurement should be&#x000a0;performed as soon as feasible during resuscitation to guide therapy. These tests also allow evaluation for underlying metabolic causes of arrest, including electrolyte derangements and severe metabolic acidosis.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Medications</bold>
</p>
        <p>Epinephrine is a 1st-line agent for nontraumatic arrest with PEA. The recommended dose is 1 mg in adults or 0.01 mg/kg, up to a maximum of 1 mg, in pediatric patients. Administration&#x000a0;may be via the intravenous or intraosseous route. Epinephrine&#x000a0;may also be given endotracheally, but the intravenous&#x000a0;or&#x000a0;intraosseous route is preferred.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r30">[30]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r31">[31]</xref></p>
        <p>Current American Heart Association (AHA) guidelines recommend against the routine use of sodium bicarbonate&#x000a0;during cardiac arrest, including in PEA cases, because evidence indicates it does not improve rates of ROSC or reduce mortality and may negatively affect neurologically intact survival.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r32">[32]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r33">[33]</xref>&#x000a0;However, sodium bicarbonate retains a role in the treatment of PEA&#x000a0;arising from hyperkalemia,&#x000a0;as this agent shifts potassium into the intracellular space. Sodium bicarbonate is also useful in cases of sodium-channel blocking agent toxicity,&#x000a0;as it provides a sodium load to compete for antagonized binding sites (eg, tricyclic antidepressants, cocaine, flecainide, citalopram). The recommended dose is 1 to 2 mEq/kg.</p>
        <p>Current AHA guidelines recommend against the routine use of parenteral calcium in cardiac arrest, including in PEA cases, because evidence suggests&#x000a0;that this intervention&#x000a0;does not improve ROSC rates and may be associated with harm.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r34">[34]</xref>&#x000a0;However, calcium should be used as an adjunctive treatment in PEA when hyperkalemia, hypermagnesemia, or&#x000a0;CCB overdose is suspected, based on history or clinical features. The typical dose is 5 to 10 mL of 10% calcium chloride solution or 15 to 30 mL of 10% calcium gluconate administered via the intravenous or intraosseous&#x000a0;route.</p>
        <p>Atropine was historically recommended for PEA treatment.&#x000a0;However, evidence indicates limited therapeutic benefit, and&#x000a0;this anticholinergic was removed from AHA ACLS guidelines in 2010.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r35">[35]</xref>&#x000a0;However, atropine retains a role in cardiac arrest caused by cholinergic toxins, including organophosphates and carbamates.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r36">[36]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Emerging and Advanced Therapies</bold>
</p>
        <p>Pseudo-PEA is an area of active clinical debate, as uncertainty persists regarding the precise definition of&#x000a0;the condition and&#x000a0;its management relative to true PEA.&#x000a0;Novel treatment protocols, such as early administration of vasopressin and cardiac compressions synchronized to the cardiac cycle, have been proposed as tailored strategies for pseudo-PEA.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r37">[37]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r38">[38]</xref></p>
        <p>The AHA recommends targeting a diastolic blood pressure greater than 25 mm Hg when an arterial line is in place, to maintain adequate cerebral perfusion pressure and maximize the likelihood of successful resuscitation. When available, extracorporeal life support may be considered for select patients with refractory PEA and favorable underlying etiologies or comorbidities.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28073.s9" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>The differential diagnosis of PEA is relatively narrow, encompassing alternative presenting cardiac rhythms in cardiac arrest, including asystole, ventricular tachycardia, and ventricular fibrillation, as determined primarily by cardiac monitoring during the arrest. Echocardiography may provide an adjunctive method to distinguish rhythms initially categorized as nonshockable PEA from fine ventricular fibrillation that may be amenable to defibrillation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r39">[39]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28073.s10" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>Prognosis for PEA is poor and generally worse than for shockable etiologies of cardiac arrest. Prognosis varies based on the duration of arrest, time to and quality of CPR, inciting etiology, and ability to correct the underlying cause.</p>
        <p>In-hospital PEA arrest studies have reported survival to hospital discharge of approximately 10% to 20%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r40">[40]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r41">[41]</xref> Two studies reported survival with a good neurologic outcome at approximately 6% and 12%, respectively.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r42">[42]</xref>Although outcomes remain poor in pediatric patients, rates of neurologically intact survival and overall survival to hospital discharge are generally higher than in adults.</p>
        <p>Outcomes for out-of-hospital PEA arrest are generally worse than those for in-hospital PEA. Rates of 30-day survival as low as 1% to 2% have been described.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r43">[43]</xref> Prognosis depends heavily on the time to high-quality CPR. In patients with witnessed PEA arrest who receive bystander CPR, survival to hospital discharge has been reported at approximately 16%, comparable to rates observed in in-hospital PEA arrest. Conversely, outcomes are dismal in cases of unwitnessed arrest without bystander CPR.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r44">[44]</xref></p>
        <p>Identification of pseudo-PEA may inform prognostication. Patients with cardiac motion on ultrasound demonstrate higher odds of ROSC than those with cardiac standstill and may have increased odds of survival to hospital discharge, although outcome data for this subset remain limited.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r45">[45]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r46">[46]</xref></p>
        <p>Outcomes for PEA in the setting of traumatic cardiac arrest are particularly poor, with survival to hospital discharge reported between 0% and 1%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r47">[47]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r48">[48]</xref> Although cardiac motion on POCUS was not associated with increased survival to hospital discharge, it has been linked to higher odds of ROSC and may provide valuable information to guide resuscitation efforts.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r49">[49]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28073.s11" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>PEA is associated with a high risk of morbidity and mortality due to prolonged systemic hypoperfusion and hypoxia. Most complications reflect those observed in cardiac arrest generally, with death representing the most severe outcome.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r50">[50]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28073.r51">[51]</xref></p>
        <p>Survivors frequently develop neurologic complications, including hypoxic brain injury, cognitive impairment, seizures, or coma. Cardiovascular complications include recurrent cardiac arrest, postresuscitation myocardial dysfunction, malignant arrhythmias, and cardiogenic shock. Pulmonary sequelae commonly observed include aspiration pneumonia, acute respiratory distress syndrome, and pulmonary edema. Renal and metabolic complications encompass acute kidney injury, severe metabolic acidosis, electrolyte abnormalities, and rhabdomyolysis. Multiorgan dysfunction may manifest as hepatic ischemia, coagulopathy, or disseminated intravascular coagulation. Resuscitative efforts can result in traumatic injuries, such as rib or sternal fractures, pneumothorax, and internal bleeding.</p>
        <p>Overall outcomes depend largely on the rapid identification and correction of reversible causes. The duration of circulatory arrest also plays a critical role in determining prognosis.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28073.s12" sec-type="Consultations">
        <title>Consultations</title>
        <p>Consultations with specialized services may assist in managing the underlying causes of PEA. Critical care services can provide ongoing management and initiate mechanical circulatory support when indicated. Trauma or acute care surgery should be involved for traumatic etiologies. Vascular surgery or cardiology consultation is appropriate if a large pulmonary embolus is detected. Toxicology consultation may guide management in cases of suspected poisoning. Cardiothoracic surgery consultation is indicated for cardiac tamponade or aortic dissection. Cardiology involvement is recommended for the comanagement of myocardial infarction and the evaluation and implementation of&#x000a0;extracorporeal membrane oxygenation in patients with potentially reversible causes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28073.s13" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Prevention of PEA focuses on early recognition and management of conditions that predispose patients to circulatory collapse. Education should emphasize the importance of managing underlying cardiovascular, pulmonary, and metabolic disorders, including heart failure, coronary artery disease, chronic lung disease, renal dysfunction, and electrolyte abnormalities. Adherence to prescribed medications, routine follow-up with healthcare providers, and prompt evaluation of worsening symptoms, such as chest pain, dyspnea, syncope, or palpitations, are essential.</p>
        <p>Patients at risk should be counseled to avoid dehydration, illicit drug use, and intake of medications that may precipitate hypotension or arrhythmias without medical supervision. Education should also include recognition of early warning signs of shock and the need for immediate activation of emergency medical services. For hospitalized or high-risk patients, families should be informed about the reversible causes of PEA and the critical importance of rapid intervention. Lifestyle modification, including smoking cessation, physical activity as tolerated, and dietary optimization, should be reinforced to reduce long-term cardiovascular risk.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28073.s14" sec-type="Pearls and Other Issues">
        <title>Pearls and Other Issues</title>
        <p>PEA&#x000a0;represents a significant subset of cardiac arrests, particularly among hospitalized,&#x000a0;older patients, and carries a high risk of mortality. High-quality CPR remains the most critical component of management. Potential causes, represented by the Hs and Ts, should be investigated during CPR and treated promptly. Epinephrine should be administered in conjunction with CPR. Adjunctive monitoring,&#x000a0;including&#x000a0;tools like POCUS or arterial line&#x000a0;waveform assessment, may assist in identifying pseudo-PEA and provide valuable information for prognostication. Best practices for managing pseudo-PEA remain an area of active clinical debate. Clinicians, especially those with limited experience in managing this condition, should follow ACLS protocols to guide initial care.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28073.s15" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>All healthcare workers, including emergency&#x000a0;medicine physicians, nurses, urgent care staff, internists, intensivists, and trauma specialists, must be trained in ACLS. Improved outcomes in patients with PEA depend on rapid recognition, high-quality CPR, and coordinated interprofessional team performance. Emergency medicine physicians, nurses, respiratory therapists, pharmacists, and paramedics must operate within a structured resuscitation framework emphasizing early identification of PEA, immediate initiation of chest compressions, and timely administration of epinephrine.</p>
        <p>Effective leadership, closed-loop communication, and clearly assigned roles reduce delays in therapy and minimize errors. Routine team-based simulation training and adherence to ACLS protocols enhance technical skills and decision-making under stress. When available, continuous waveform capnography and bedside ultrasound should be integrated to assess CPR quality and identify reversible causes such as cardiac tamponade, massive pulmonary embolism, or hypovolemia.</p>
        <p>Postresuscitation care requires collaboration with intensive care specialists to optimize hemodynamics, ventilation, and neurologic protection. Ongoing quality improvement initiatives, including debriefing after resuscitation events and review of response times and protocol compliance, further strengthen team performance and contribute to improved survival and neurologic outcomes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28073.s16">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
